        <h2>Summary / Description</h2>
        <div class="description">
          OBJECTIVES:<br>
This work grew out of earth applications for gaseous fuels, particularly natural gas. Better understanding of acoustic properties of gaseous mixtures permits the potential for the development of real-time gaseous process control monitors or alarm monitors in a wide range of applications including hazardous gas detection, industrial process control, and environmental monitoring. This research will lead to better theoretical models of acoustic properties in gases, thus laying the groundwork for advancing the applications of acoustic sensors. In addition, the methods used to solve the "inverse problem" of extracting the gas composition from acoustic measurements may be applicable in other situations.
<p>
The project concerns the development of an acoustic sensor for the reliable monitoring of gas concentrations in on-board environmental control and life-support systems such as the closed-loop oxygen recovery system. In parallel to the development of the actual sensor, theoretical work is in progress aimed at predicting the acoustic attenuation and sound speed in gas mixtures as well as solving the inverse problem of identifying contaminant concentrations. The methodology has been to extend the model of acoustic attenuation to account for hydrogen, oxygen, and carbon dioxide (methane and water vapor are already included in the model). The results of "simulations" of acoustic attenuation under a variety of operating conditions (temperature, pressure, composition) provides information necessary to determine the potential for this approach to process control monitoring or humidity measurement. A small number of experiments have also been performed by an outside acoustics lab to confirm the acoustic attenuation analysis. The objective of this project was to develop a real-time flow-through acoustic sensor to monitor gaseous processes in on-board environmental systems, along with an accurate predictive model. A critical function of life-support systems on a spacecraft is maintaining the air fresh and of nominal composition. Air revitalization involves many situations that will benefit from reliable gaseous process control sensors, such as replenishing of oxygen, removing of excess carbon-dioxide, monitoring air composition, and monitoring humidity levels. Gases that need to be monitored at the nodes of an air revitalization system are oxygen, nitrogen, carbon-dioxide, methane, hydrogen, water vapor, and their mixtures. Another use for reliable sensors is monitoring of the atmospheres of hydroponics chambers, since plant growth depends on nominal gas composition. Acoustic gas sensors are very good candidates for these tasks since they are rugged, virtually calibration- and maintenance-free and give real-time output.
<p>
APPROACH:<br>
The methodology for the study isn’t available.
<p>
RESULTS:<br>
The GAS 1 prototype has been developed at Commercial Electronics.  The acoustic impedance of the gas cannot be measured independently of attenuation and sound speed in typical transmit-receive setups. Extra knowledge is needed of either the particle velocity in the sensing volume, measurable with particle velocity sensors or the surface displacement of the piezoelectric transmitter, measurable with either very near-field particle velocity sensors or digital laser Doppler vibrometers.  The investigators have developed an inversion algorithm that is able to infer the nature of contaminant molecules based on operation at only two frequencies. 
<p>
        </div>

